          "snippet": "Introduction.-Optical trapping has broad applications among researchers after nearly 40 years of development [1,2]. Examples include simple manipulation of nanoparticles and cells [3][4][5], precise measurements of piconewton forces and nanometer or smaller displacements in biological systems [6][7][8][9], and three-dimensional imaging of polymer networks [10]. In optical tweezers, a laser beam is focused by a high numerical aperture objective lens to a diffraction-limited spot in which two types of forces act: a stabilizing gradient force that results from the intensity gradient and points towards higher intensity and a destabilizing scattering force that points along the propagation direction of the light (optical axis).",
